    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "4 families. Positions 6 and 7 contain 1 and 2, so six families own at most one car; $6-2=4$.",
      "worked": "The median position is $(12+1)\\div2=6.5$, so use observations 6 and 7.\n\nTheir values must be 1 and 2, since $(1+2)\\div2=1.5$. The other possible pair, 0 and 3, is ruled out because only the first two observations are zero.\n\nPosition 6 is the last one-car family and position 7 is the first two-car family. Exactly six families therefore own at most one car.\n\n$6-2=4$ families own one car.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 35
